cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  Lyrebird: a physically interpretable syllable inventory for
  2,650 wild bird species
message: >-
  If you use this work, please cite it. If you use the
  recordings behind it, cite the recordists — every one is
  named in the store's CREDITS.md, and their licences are
  their own.
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Shahab
    family-names: Nedaei
    affiliation: variable.gallery
    email: ned.tabulov@gmail.com
    # orcid: 'https://orcid.org/0000-0000-0000-0000'   <- add yours
repository-code: 'https://github.com/sha5b/Lyrebird'
abstract: >-
  Lyrebird measures syllables in wild bird recordings from
  xeno-canto and iNaturalist. It fits the Laje-Mindlin
  normal-form syrinx oscillator to each measured syllable type
  by coordinate descent, learns a per-species type inventory
  and a first-order grammar over it, and ships the result as a
  browser application. The pipeline decodes each recording,
  describes it as numbers, and deletes it: no audio is
  redistributed and no model weights reach the browser. Fifty
  eight gates were registered before their runs and thirty six
  ended in a written refusal, which are as much of the
  contribution as the system is.
keywords:
  - bioacoustics
  - birdsong
  - syrinx model
  - gesture inversion
  - contrastive embeddings
  - sequence statistics
  - recordist leakage
  - evaluation methodology
  - negative results
  - pre-registration
license: CC-BY-NC-SA-4.0
